= Inductive reasoning — Technical =

== Ampliation ==
An inference is ampliative when the conclusion contains information not deductively contained in the premises.

Therefore:

<pre>
premises true
does not entail
conclusion true
</pre>

even when the inference is inductively strong.

== Probabilistic support ==
A conditional probability can represent degree of support:

<pre>
P(H | E)
</pre>

where H is a hypothesis and E evidence.

High conditional probability does not by itself settle questions about priors, model specification, sampling, or causal interpretation.

== Enumerative induction ==
A simple schema is:

<pre>
observed A₁...Aₙ are B
therefore other/all A are probably B
</pre>

Reliability depends on sampling and population structure.

== Inductive logic ==
Formal inductive logics attempt to characterize degrees of evidential support rather than only truth-preserving consequence.

Bayesian systems are prominent but not the only approach.

